WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.640
Have you ever wondered

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.560
if there are more natural ways
to lower your high blood pressure,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.520
guard against Alzheimer's,
lose weight, and feel better?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.720
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.800
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.320
Today we look at some new research
on one of our favorite beverages

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.160
and we start with how to
choose the healthiest coffee.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.800
Coffee may have beneficial
effects on inflammation,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.920
lung function, insulin
sensitivity, and depression.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.680
This may be in part because of a
class of polyphenol phytonutrients

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.800
found in coffee beans called chlorogenic
acids, proven to have favorable effects

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.440
with studies like these where
they just give it alone in pill form

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.720
and can show beneficial effects, such as
acute blood pressure-lowering activity,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.080
dropping the top and bottom blood pressure
numbers within hours of consumption.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.320
OK, so which coffee has the most?

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.880
We know how to choose the reddest tomato,
the deepest orange sweet potato,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.520
since many of the plant pigments
are the antioxidants themselves.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.560
How do you choose
the healthiest coffee?

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.800
More than a hundred coffees were tested,
and different coffees had different

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.240
caffeine levels, but the chlorogenic
acid levels varied by more than 30-fold.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.520
As a consequence, coffee selection
may have a large influence

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.720
on the potential health
potential of coffee intake.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.800
So all those studies that show that
one cup of coffee does this or that,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.280
what does that even mean
when coffee can vary so greatly?

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.120
Interestingly, the major
contributor to the wide range

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.680
was the coffee
purchased from Starbucks,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.120
which had an extremely
low chlorogenic acid content,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.160
averaging 10 times
lower than the others.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.840
Maybe it's because they
roast their beans too dark.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.560
The more you roast,
the less there is.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.920
They appear to be partially
destroyed by roasting.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.640
Caffeine is pretty stable, but a dark
roast may wipe out nearly 90%

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.000
of the chlorogenic acid
content of the beans.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.200
The difference between a medium
light roast and a medium roast

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.560
was not enough though to make a
difference in total antioxidant status

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.560
in people's bloodstreams
after drinking them;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.960
they both gave about
the same boost.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.600
Other factors, such as how
you prepare it or decaffeination,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.160
don't appear to have a major effect.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.880
What about adding milk?

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.320
Long-time fans may remember
this ancient video,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.000
where the addition of milk was shown

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.560
to prevent the protective effects
of tea on artery function.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.880
Drink black tea and you get
a significant improvement

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.360
in vascular function within hours,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.000
whereas addition of milk completely
blunted the effects of tea.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.080
Here's the big boost in artery
function you get drinking tea,

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.600
but drink the same amount
of tea with milk and it's like

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.640
you never drank the tea at all.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.640
They think it's the casein to blame,

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.160
one of the milk proteins binding
up the tea phytonutrients.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.160
Bottom-line, this finding that the tea-
induced improvement in vascular function

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.320
is completely attenuated after addition
of milk may have broad implications

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.440
on the mode of tea preparation
and consumption.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.680
In other words, maybe we should not add
milk to tea or put cream on our berries.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.640
It appears to have the same effect
on berry phytonutrients, or chocolate.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:25.840
Check this out.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.160
Eat milk chocolate,
and nothing much happens

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.240
to the antioxidant power
of your bloodstream.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.840
But eat dark chocolate and get a nice
spike within an hour of consumption.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.480
Yeah, but is that just because
the milk in milk chocolate

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.280
crowds out some of the
antioxidant-rich cocoa?

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.000
Milk chocolate may
only be like 20% cocoa,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.880
whereas a good dark chocolate may
be like 70% or more cocoa solids.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.640
No, it's not just that.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.800
Here's how much of this
cocoa phytonutrient

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.920
you get into your bloodstream

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.600
eating dark chocolate
compared to milk chocolate.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.840
But eat that same amount
of dark chocolate

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.760
with a glass of milk and
it blocks about half.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.240
OK, that's cocoa beans;
what about coffee beans?

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.520
When milk was added to
the coffee in like a test tube,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.920
antioxidant activity decreased by more
than half with just a splash of milk,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.640
and down like 95% in latte
or something with lots of milk.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.800
OK, but what happens in a test tube
doesn't necessarily happen in a person.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.960
I mean, you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.200
And indeed, over the course of a day
significantly fewer chlorogenic acids

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.360
made it into people's bloodstreams
drinking coffee with milk

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.760
compared to black, cutting
absorption by more than half.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.360
What about soymilk?

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.840
In a test tube, coffee phytonutrients do
appear to bind not only to dairy proteins

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.320
but also egg and soy proteins.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.800
You can see how they did
this computer modeling

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.000
showing how these coffee compounds
can dock inside the nooks and crannies

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.480
of dairy, egg white, and soy proteins,

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.600
but what happens in a test
tube or a computer simulation

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.640
doesn't necessarily
happen in a person.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.240
Eggs haven't been put to the test,
so you don't know if having omelets

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.120
with your black coffee
could impair absorption,

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:17.040
and neither has soymilk until now.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.080
Yeah, either way soymilk has some
inherent benefits over cow's milk,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.120
but does it have the same
nutrient blocking effects?

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.480
And the answer is no.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.680
No significant difference in the
absorption of coffee phytonutrients

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.120
drinking coffee black or with soymilk.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.920
What seemed to be happening
is that the soy proteins

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.600
do initially bind the coffee compounds
up in the small intestine,

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.040
but then your good bacteria
can release them so they can be

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.280
absorbed down in the lower intestine.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.600
So considering the reversible nature of
binding as opposed to the dairy proteins,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.280
it seems not to be as relevant as
to whether or not you add soy milk.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.240
In our next story,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.000
we explore why coffee may
not be for everyone.

00:06:05.681 --> 00:06:08.720
Three-quarters of American
adults drink coffee,

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.440
about half of which
on a daily basis.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.760
That comes out to a million
tons of coffee every year.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.200
Might there be "grounds" for concern?

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.240
Population studies have found
that coffee drinkers tend to have

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.040
lower risk of Parkinson's, less prostate
cancer (especially the women),

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:32.320
less liver cancer, less diabetes, liver
cirrhosis, depression among women,

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.400
and a reduction in mortality overall, such
that coffee drinkers tend to live longer

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.600
than non-coffee drinkers, with mortality
bottoming out at about four cups a day.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.320
But these are all just associations.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:52.000
You don't know if it's cause and
effect until you put it to the test.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.840
For example, coffee really
does seem to protect the liver.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.120
Take people with chronic hepatitis
and have them drink coffee, or not,

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.000
for a month, then
switch them back.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.440
And the coffee really
did seem to help.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.360
Similarly, randomize Parkinson's patients
to get two cups of coffee's worth

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.680
of caffeine and get a
significant improvement

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.800
in movement symptoms
within three weeks.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.920
Runners, randomized to drink coffee,
shaved about six seconds off their mile.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.960
Weight lifters, randomized to
coffee, can squat more weight,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.440
about 600 pounds more worth of reps.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.240
And not just athletic performance.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:34.800
A cup of coffee's worth of caffeine
can significantly improve IELT,

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.440
which stands for "intravaginal
ejaculation latency time,"

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.840
from two minutes all
the way up to five.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.320
Unfortunately, those effectively
randomized at birth to genetically

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.120
just have a higher
predilection to drink coffee

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.480
do not appear to be protected
from diabetes, or depression,

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.360
or Alzheimer's, or obesity,
or metabolic syndrome.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.080
So it seems the protective associations
may just be due to confounding factors,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.920
like maybe those who drink coffee just
happen to exercise more or something.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:11.200
And the same with prostate cancer: no
apparent cause-and-effect relationship,

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.560
and even the mortality benefit
seemed to disappear.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.600
Even if a study did show
coffee drinking could

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.400
extend lifespan by reducing
inflammation or improving

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.000
lung function, or insulin sensitivity,
that would mean on average.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.080
There is "inter-individual variability"
after consumption

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.760
of major plant-food compounds,
meaning people may respond differently.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:44.560
For example, how crazy is this: in some
rare individuals, heavy use of caffeine

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.560
apparently provokes sleepiness.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.320
That's an extreme example.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:54.240
Most of the time it's just that some
individuals may benefit more than others

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.480
from the health effects
of different foods.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:02.560
For example, because of differences in gut
bacteria, only a minority of Westerners

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.680
may derive extra benefit from soy,
as I've described before.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.240
The most common difference in caffeine
effects is that, while most people

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:18.400
metabolize caffeine rapidly, certain
gene variants in liver detox enzymes

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.600
make some people slow metabolizers.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.480
Might that make a difference?

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.160
Well, habitual coffee consumption
of at least three cups a day

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.480
has been associated with uncontrolled
blood pressure among older individuals

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.320
diagnosed with hypertension,
suggesting that moderating

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.840
coffee intake may be a
good idea for some people.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.680
But even if it was cause-and-effect,
that would be on average.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.400
What would happen if you split people up
by how fast they metabolize caffeine?

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.640
Compared to coffee abstainers, those who
have impaired caffeine metabolism genes

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.520
have an elevated risk of becoming
hypertensive at one to three cups a day,

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.960
and especially at four or more.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.880
OK, but check this out.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.760
For the rapid metabolizers not
only was there no excess risk

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:16.160
at one to three cups, heavy coffee
drinkers were protected,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.080
meaning apparently
the more coffee they drank,

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:23.760
the lower their risk.
How do we explain that?

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:28.160
Coffee is a complex blend of a vast
number of different compounds.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.840
There are protective polyphenol
antioxidants that are beneficial.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:34.320
Coffee beans are, after all, beansâ€”

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.600
well, actually, seeds, but
seeds are really healthy too.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.360
On the other hand, there's the caffeine,
which can spike adrenaline levels

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.080
in the blood, but only if
you're a slow metabolizer.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.280
Rapid metabolizers can
clear caffeine so fast

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.440
that there's no increase in adrenaline
even at four or more cups a day.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.800
And so then you just have the
beneficial polyphenols that actually

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.360
lower your blood pressure,
hence the benefit overall.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.800
Thus, there seems to be a
Jekyll and Hyde aspect to coffee

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.920
whose overall action on the
cardiovascular system appears

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.560
to be regulated by that
gene for the enzyme

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.440
that metabolizes caffeine.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.960
The important question, though, is
does it give women larger breasts?

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.480
W-h-a-t?

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.240
Young women who drink a lot of coffee
and are rapid caffeine metabolizers

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:28.240
have about a half-a-baseball-sized
larger breast volume, which may be

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.480
a bad thing as breast volume is
associated with breast cancer risk.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:38.240
But no, the important question is
what about heart attack risk?

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.080
In slow metabolizers, daily coffee
consumption appeared to double

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:46.160
the odds of a heart attack, or even
quadruple the odds at four cups a day,

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:50.160
whereas in the rapid caffeine
metabolizers, daily coffee consumption

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.720
was protective, cutting the odds of
a heart attack by more than half,

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:58.400
or at least until you get up
to four or more cups a day.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:02.240
The protective effects observed
among rapid metabolizers suggest

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:06.640
that the efficient elimination of
caffeine might have unmasked

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.040
the protective effects of other
phytochemicals in coffee.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:13.680
We think it may be
the adrenaline again,

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:18.720
since if you're a slow metabolizer of
adrenaline, high coffee consumption

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.000
may put you at risk as well.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.960
So, is coffee friend or foe?

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:29.280
These studies suggest the possibility
that slow caffeine metabolizers

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.480
who consume caffeinated coffee
may have an increased risk

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:33.920
of cardiovascular disease,

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.160
whereas fast caffeine metabolizers
may be protected from this risk

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:43.040
by the antioxidants and other
beneficial compounds present.

